  "known_differences_from_fetch_script": [
    "Coordinates: the script (01_fetch_nola.py:208-237) parses lat/lon per row from the Socrata `location` column, which has two shapes (raw_cache.parquet schema: struct<coordinates: list<double>, human_address, latitude: string, longitude: string, type>): 2018-2019 rows carry legacy `latitude`/`longitude` strings with `coordinates` null; 2020-2024 rows carry a GeoJSON Point `coordinates` [lon, lat] with latitude/longitude null. The revised contract uses `location#point` for both coordinate fields, which reads either shape per row (GeoJSON `coordinates` or Socrata `latitude`/`longitude`), and `require_coordinates` drops nulls and (0,0) as the script does (01_fetch_nola.py:231-232, 239-242). Counted on raw_cache.parquet by applying the per-row shape test to every row: coordinates-shaped 156,930 (all of the 2020-2024 files), latitude/longitude-shaped 65,087 (2018: 30,376; 2019: 34,711), non-dict 16 (2018: 14; 2019: 2), neither shape 348 (2019); 0 rows carry both shapes, so the order in which the runner checks the two shapes cannot change the result. The previous contract's `location.latitude` failed because the runner does not resolve dotted struct paths (check error: KeyError 'none of location.latitude in the pull'). A live Socrata pull returns `location` as the same two JSON shapes by dataset, which `#point` also reads.",
    "Block-group join: the script left-joins (01_fetch_nola.py:262) and keeps points that fall in no Louisiana 2020 block group. One kept row does: nopd_item H0995923 (2023 file, type_ VEHSTO, coordinates [-90.47422562, 28.49213913], offshore in the Gulf; README.md:130-131). If the runner drops unmatched points, it keeps 197,688 rows instead of 197,689.",
    "De-duplication: the script de-duplicates on incident_id (= nopd_item) after the spatial join (01_fetch_nola.py:266); the contract de-duplicates on the id after the coordinate filter and before the join. On the archived pull this changes nothing: 343 nopd_item values appear twice (once in the 2019 file, once in the 2020 file, identical type_ and disposition, timecreate in 2020); every 2019-file copy has only a garbled `location.human_address` and no lat/lon, so the coordinate filter drops it and de-duplication removes 0 rows in the replay.",
    "Live-pull where clause: the script sent only `type_ in(<70 codes>)` to each per-year dataset (01_fetch_nola.py:143-144) and took the whole file; the contract adds a timecreate range with {start}/{end}. The script's year filter (2018-2024 on the year of timecreate, 01_fetch_nola.py:244-247) is reproduced by the runner's year step. Rows whose timecreate falls in a different year than their source file (348 rows in the 2019 file with timecreate in 2020) are not lost by the range because year assignment comes from timecreate in both.",
    "The script adds a computed `source_year_file` column (01_fetch_nola.py:177) and renames output columns (policedistrict->district, type_->signal_type, typetext->signal_description; 01_fetch_nola.py:277-279); neither affects which rows are kept or the id."
  ],
